	title = {The {{Logic}} of {{Lyric}} ({{Part One}})},
	url = {https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-12314-6_7},
	urldate = {2024-02-01},
	abstract = {This book takes lyric poetry as the exemplary context in which to conduct the legitimation of poetic reason it offers. In this and the following chapter I survey lyric poetry in two radically distinct contexts and identify an underlying logic of lyric that exemplifies the structures of poetic reason which must be legitimated. In the context of early Vedic poetry, I look at a range of scholars who collectively help to identify what I call the esoteric function and the role that it plays in Vedic lyric in particular. In the context of American lyric poetry (Dickinson, Frost, Stevens and Ashbery) I read individual poems with an eye to how this esoteric function is enlisted. In Dickinson the esoteric function leads to a practice of esoteric poetry, whereas in Frost and Ashbery, the esoteric function is co-opted by gnostic tendencies. The case of Stevens is more equivocal and so helps to see the complexity of the issues at stake.}}

