	url = {https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-05817-2_2},
	urldate = {2024-02-19},
	abstract = {In a short but quite informative text on the reception of Heidegger's phenomenology in North America, Thomas Sheehan, a well-known philosopher and commentator of Heidegger's works, underlines that, in opposition to Husserl, Heidegger ``was late arriving in America'' since it was only in the 1960s that ``the true nature of Heidegger's project became clear to American scholars''. Only a few of Heidegger's texts had been translated into English until then, among them What is Metaphysics?, On the Essence of Truth, in the 1940s, Heidegger's 1935 course on The Introduction to Metaphysics in the late 1950s, and Identity and Difference and The Question of Being in 1960s. During this period, the reading of Heidegger's works was dominated by what Thomas Sheehan names ``the existentialist paradigm'' which focused on the thematic of human existence. But things changed after the publication of the English translation of Sein und Zeit, Kant und das Problem der Metaphysik, Platons Lehre von der Wahrheit and Brief {\"u}ber den Humanismus in 1962, since the emphasis was now put on being itself and no longer on human being.}}

