	url = {https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/50/article/651903},
	urldate = {2024-02-03},
	volume = {38},
	abstract = {400 BOOK REVIEWS/COMPTES RENDUS to range very freely (some might say too freely) throughout classical literature and beyond in their efforts to understand various aspects of ancient Greek culture and society. Lang and Willcock have both written articles about myth, example, and allusion in the Iliad; S. cites both in her bibliography, and uses Lang's word 'reverberation' at a key point in her conclusion (l08). What is new about S.'s work is her focus on Thetis and the important implications her characterization has for the meaning of the Iliad. Both books are successful in provoking the reader to look with a keener eye at discrete elements within the Iliad. One can no longer dismiss the lion similes as ornamental effect; one can no longer ignore the significance of Achilles's mother in the shaping of his fate and the meaning of the Iliad. Both books do unfortunately reveal limitations which are due to the critical approach of the author. L. leaves the reader hoping for something beyond the strict focus on the many textual examples of lion similes: what does it mean in the Iliad for heroes to be compared repeatedly to vicious animals? Is there any significance in the frequent depiction of death in the lion similes? S., on the other hand, at times seems to depart too widely from meaning that can be derived from the text, especially in the chapter on Thetis and Demeter. L.'s book could have profited by an expanded critical horizon, S.'s by a somewhat more rigorous strategy. INGRID E. HOLMBERG DEPARlMENT OF CLASSICS UNIVERSITY OF VICIDRIA VICTORIA, BC V8W 3P4 LOUISE PRAIT. Lying and Poetry from Homer to Pindar: Falsehood and Deception in Archaic Greek Poetics. Ann Arbor: University of Michigan Press, 1993. pp. 180. ISBN 0-472-10417-9. Louise Pratt's Lying and Poetry from Homer to Pindar is a thoughtprovoking first book. In a revision of her exemplary dissertation (University of Michigan 1988), P. proposes that the earliest poets of Greece share Vladimir Nab