erts his desire 'to speak forth true tllings [aLethea]', he is concealing from Eumaeus his knowledge tllat tlle beggar is Odysseus and inventing a plausible excuse to get Odysseus back to his palace. Yet P. rightly condemns tlle general assumption in scholarship tllat archaic poetry's primary concern is witll aLetheia, a tenn associated witll poetry's function only once before tlle fifUl century (Theogony...}}

