	urldate = {2024-02-22},
	abstract = {The chapter surveys the field of Archaic Greek literature, poetry in particular. It argues for the need to understand the various genres of Archaic poetry and song---lyric, rhapsody, and citharody---as communicative media intended for live performance on socially meaningful occasions. Typical occasions for performance---symposia, contests, festivals---are reviewed, as are the social, religious, and political contexts in which songs and song traditions took shape and had meaning: aristocratic factionalism, tyrannical patronage, panhellenic festival culture, local cults and rituals. Special attention is paid to the formative influence of Lydian culture on Archaic poetic texts and their musical performance.}}

