	url = {https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-05817-2_4},
	urldate = {2024-02-19},
	abstract = {This essay engages themes and core questions marking the readings of Heidegger by some Anglo-American deconstructive thinkers. The first section titled ``Radical finitude, abyssal truth, and radical questioning'' sets out with a brief presentation of the threat posed by Derridean deconstruction with respect to more ``orthodox'' interpretations of Heidegger's thought and proceeds with an account of Charles Scott's reading of death in Being and Time and then an account of John Sallis' reading of Heidegger's ``On the Essence of Truth.'' The second section, titled ``The history of being and transgressing metaphysics,'' problematizes Heidegger's thinking of the history of being also with reference to critiques by Anglo-American thinkers. The third section ``Disseminations'' joins critiques put forward by Scott, David Farrell Krell, and David Wood with respect to a resistance against dissemination in Heidegger's thought and points to the project of thinking time without a unifying origin.}}

